==============================================================================
PART A: independent symbolic re-derivation of the Poisson closed form
==============================================================================
phi_n(x) := T_prof(x/sqrt(n), gamma) = exp(x**2*(gamma - 2)/(2*gamma*n))/gamma
int_{-inf}^{inf} phi_n(x) dx [full-line Gaussian, cited] = sqrt(2)*sqrt(pi)*sqrt(n)/(sqrt(gamma)*sqrt(2 - gamma))
int_0^{inf} phi_n(x) dx = (by evenness, Part B) half of that = sqrt(2)*sqrt(pi)*sqrt(n)/(2*sqrt(gamma)*sqrt(2 - gamma))
phi_n_hat(k) = sqrt(2)*sqrt(pi)*sqrt(n)*exp(2*pi**2*gamma*k**2*n/(gamma - 2))/(sqrt(gamma)*sqrt(2 - gamma))
Consistency check phi_n_hat(0) / (full-line integral) [PASS if 1] = 1
Boundary term phi_n(0)/2 = 1/(2*gamma)  [claimed: 1/(2*gamma)]
Rate constant c(gamma) := pi^2/alpha = -2*pi**2*gamma/(gamma - 2)  [claimed: 2*pi^2*gamma/(2-gamma)]

Independent re-derivation CONFIRMS the front's closed form exactly:
  sum_{m=0}^inf phi_n(m) = int_0^inf phi_n dx + 1/(2*gamma)
                            + sum_{k=1}^inf phi_n_hat(k),
  phi_n_hat(k) = (1/gamma) sqrt(pi n / alpha) exp(-pi^2 k^2 n / alpha),
  dominant k=1 term ~ (1/gamma) sqrt(pi n/alpha) exp(-c(gamma) n),
  c(gamma) = 2 pi^2 gamma / (2-gamma).

==============================================================================
PART B: evenness of T_prof(lambda,gamma) on ALL of R -- independent check
==============================================================================

T_prof(lambda,gamma) = (1/gamma) exp[-((2-gamma)/(2*gamma)) * lambda^2] is,
AS A CLOSED-FORM EXPRESSION, manifestly a function of lambda ONLY through
lambda^2 -- so T_prof(-lambda,gamma) = T_prof(lambda,gamma) is an immediate
algebraic consequence, not requiring any delicate branch-cut or domain
argument.  This is checked below both by direct symbolic substitution and
by confirming there is no hidden sqrt(lambda) / lambda^(odd) / log(lambda)
dependence anywhere in the closed form (which WOULD make lambda -> -lambda
ill-defined or multivalued).

Using a symbol declared REAL (not positive) -- genuinely testing lambda<0:
T_prof(lambda,gamma) - T_prof(-lambda,gamma), simplified = 0
Taylor series of T_prof in lambda about 0, odd-power coefficients (orders 1,3,...,11):
  [0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0]
All odd coefficients vanish -- confirms T_prof is entire and even on ALL of R,
not merely 'even by symmetry of the formula' but genuinely single-valued and
analytic for lambda<0 too (no sqrt/log/lambda^(non-integer) term present).

Subtlety check: does extending phi_n from {physical m=0,1,2,...} to all of
Z via the CLOSED-FORM T_prof (not the actual combinatorial term_m, which
has no meaning for m<0) introduce any inconsistency?

NO -- the Poisson-summation manipulation operates ENTIRELY on the analytic
proxy phi_n(x) = T_prof(x/sqrt(n),gamma), which by construction (an explicit
elementary Gaussian formula) is defined and Schwartz-class for ALL real x,
with no reference to the combinatorial meaning of term_m(n,gamma) at
negative-integer m (which is genuinely undefined / meaningless). This is a
purely ANALYTIC extension of a closed-form function, not a claim that
'term_{-1}(n,gamma)' means anything combinatorially. The front's own
decomposition (Part C above) never evaluates phi_n at a negative integer and
attaches combinatorial meaning to it -- phi_n(-m) for m>0 is used only as an
intermediate bookkeeping device inside the identity
sum_{m in Z} phi_n(m) = phi_n(0) + 2 sum_{m=1}^inf phi_n(m), which is valid
purely because phi_n(-m)=phi_n(m) as ANALYTIC values of the SAME closed-form
function, regardless of whether "term_{-m}" would mean anything. No
subtlety or gap found here.


